ces around you but  measuring force first requires knowledge  that your frame is inertial which  requires measuring for forces you know  there's a circle or okay obviously that  won't work well there's a second less  precise method which is just observing  the majority of matter and energy around  you and making sure you're not  accelerating relative to that for  example when you spin in a circle all  the stars and universe spin relative to  you that's a pretty good sign that your  frame is not an inertial one and that  the centrifugal forces pulling your  limbs outwards aren't real thus to be  confident of the presence of a force  requires multiple observations not just  of whatever matter is experiencing the  acceleration but also of the surrounding  environment and it's  this is what so many people get wrong  about force they're taught to couple it  blindly to acceleration when in fact  these are two vastly different  phenomenon acceleration is measurable  and always relative to the observer  while force which hinges on one's  knowledge of the total system is  epistemic and inferred indirectly this  means you can never feel force not in  any sense of the word indeed the more  you examine force the less tangible it  becomes not only does the reality of it  differ widely based on context but one  entire fundamental force gravity was  shown by Einstein to be something else  altogether an emergent property of the  geometry of space-time quantum mechanics  meanwhile doesn't even bother with the  concept of force but even before modern  physics the ontological status of force  was already well being called into  question philosophers and physicists the  likes of Lazar a Carnot Gustav Kirchhoff  Henri Poincare a and others were so  bothered by the unobservable unreality  of force that they all independently  attempted to construct a framework for  classical mechanics that did not invoke  the concept of force but rather relied  on observables alone what bothered panca  R